working-directory: web
run: npm run build
- name: Configure AWS credentials (OIDC)
uses: aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@v4
with:
role-to-assume: arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/github-frontend-deploy
aws-region: ap-southeast-2
- name: Upload immutable assets first
run: |
aws s3 sync web/dist/assets s3://my-web-bucket/assets --cache-control "public,max-age=31536000,immutable" --delete
- name: Upload index.html last
run: |
aws s3 cp web/dist/index.html s3://my-web-bucket/index.html --cache-control "public,max-age=60,s-maxage=60"
- name: Invalidate CloudFront index
run: |
aws cloudfront create-invalidation --distribution-id E1234567890ABC --paths "/index.html" "/"
Why this pipeline pattern is safe
- immutable assets are uploaded first
- release pointer (
index.html
) is updated last - invalidation is small and targeted (fast and cheaper than
/*
invalidations every time)
Example backend rollout approach (Lambda alias + CodeDeploy)
I usually avoid flipping production traffic to a fresh Lambda version all at once.
Safer rollout design
- Publish new Lambda version
- Route traffic through alias (for example
live
) - Use canary deployment:
- 10% for 5 minutes
- then 100% if alarms stay healthy
Alarms I wire to rollback
- API Gateway 5XX errors
- Lambda errors
- Lambda duration p95/p99 (if latency-sensitive)
- Custom business error metrics (best signal if available)
Why this matters
This catches bad releases before they impact all users and gives me confidence to deploy frequently.
End-to-end implementation walkthrough (build order I would actually follow)
Here is the practical sequence I recommend.
Step 1: Provision domains, certificates, and DNS
- Register/manage domain in Route 53
- Create ACM certificate(s)
- Plan hostnames:
app.example.com
api.example.com
- Decide whether API is a single domain or split by bounded context
Step 2: Build frontend hosting (S3 + CloudFront)
- Create private S3 bucket
- Create CloudFront distribution with OAC
- Add SPA rewrite CloudFront Function
- Configure cache policies:
- short TTL for
index.html
- long TTL for hashed assets
- short TTL for
- Attach WAF (CloudFront scope)
Step 3: Set up Cognito
- Create User Pool and App Client
- Configure PKCE auth flow
- Set callback/logout URLs to
app.example.com
- Add required claims (for example tenant/role custom claims if needed)
Step 4: Build API layer (API Gateway + Lambda)
- Create API Gateway (HTTP API or REST API based on feature needs)
- Add Cognito/JWT authorizer
- Implement Lambda handlers
- Wire in CORS to only allow
https://app.example.com
Step 5: Add DynamoDB
- Define table and indexes around access patterns
- Add least-privilege IAM permissions for Lambda
- Enable PITR (Point-in-Time Recovery)
Step 6: Add WAF and throttling
- WAF managed rules + rate-based rules
- API Gateway throttling defaults and route overrides
- Optional Lambda reserved concurrency on expensive functions
Step 7: Add observability
- Structured logging in Lambda
- CloudWatch dashboards
- Alarms for 4xx/5xx, throttles, Lambda errors, latency
- Optional X-Ray tracing
Step 8: Build CI/CD and rollback controls
- Separate frontend and backend pipelines
- Immutable artifacts
- Lambda versions/aliases/canary rollout
- Automated rollback on alarms
- Smoke tests after deploy
At this point, the architecture is not just “serverless.” It is operationally reliable.
Implementation discussion and production tradeoffs
This is where I usually help teams avoid overengineering early without painting themselves into a corner.
HTTP API vs REST API in API Gateway
I often choose HTTP API for browser-backed web apps because it is simpler and cost-effective, especially when JWT auth is straightforward.
I choose REST API when I specifically need features like:
- usage plans / API keys for metering
- request/response transformations in legacy scenarios
- certain advanced API Gateway features not required by the app path
The architecture stays mostly the same. The operational controls change slightly.
CloudFront in front of API or not?
For many web apps, I keep:
- CloudFront only for frontend (
app.example.com
) - API Gateway directly for
api.example.com
This keeps API behavior explicit and reduces caching surprises.
I put CloudFront in front of APIs only when I have a strong reason (for example global API edge caching for public GET endpoints, unified WAF strategy, or latency tuning for specific patterns).
DynamoDB modeling approach
I do not force a single-table design if the app is still small and access patterns are not stable. I absolutely use DynamoDB in production, but I choose the data model based on real query requirements.
The production-grade principle here is not “single-table at all costs.” It is:
- predictable access patterns
- correct indexes
- safe migrations
- observability for throttles/hot keys
CORS discipline
I set CORS precisely:
- allow only
https://app.example.com
- only needed methods/headers
- no wildcard origins for authenticated production apps
It is a small detail that prevents a lot of confusion later.
Common mistakes I see (and how I avoid them)
1) Public S3 bucket for the website
This is a legacy shortcut.
What I do instead: private S3 + CloudFront Origin Access Control (OAC).
2) Caching index.html
like immutable assets
This causes stale releases and confusing user behavior.
What I do instead: short TTL for index.html
, long TTL for hashed assets.
3) One giant API with no domain boundaries
This becomes hard to deploy, test, and secure as the team grows.
What I do instead: separate by capability/domain (hostnames or base paths).
4) JWT validation only, no business authorization
A valid token does not equal permission to perform an action.
What I do instead: Lambda checks roles/tenant/resource ownership.
5) WAF only, no API throttling
WAF is excellent, but not a replacement for endpoint-level throttling and backend protection.
What I do instead: layered controls (WAF + API Gateway throttling + Lambda concurrency limits where needed).
6) No rollback plan for data shape changes
This is where “quick deploys” become incidents.
What I do instead: additive DynamoDB changes, feature flags, and staged migrations.
Final thoughts
A production-grade serverless web app architecture on AWS is not about using more services. It is about choosing clear boundaries and safe defaults.
For me, the winning combination is:
- CloudFront + S3 for fast, cacheable frontend delivery
- API Gateway + Lambda for clean, independently deployable backend logic
- DynamoDB for low-latency scale
- Cognito for standardized auth
- WAF + throttling for layered protection
- Separate pipelines + rollback design for operational confidence
If I get the caching strategy, auth boundary, and deployment rollback model right early, the architecture remains easy to evolve as the app grows.
